    <!-- FAQ Section -->
    <section class="py-12 lg:py-16">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
        <h2
          class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 mb-8 text-center"
        >
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <details
            class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg shadow-sm overflow-hidden group border border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-700"
          >
            <summary
              class="px-6 py-4 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-200 flex justify-between items-center hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-700/50"
            >
              How do I calculate projector throw distance?
              <svg
                class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 transform group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              >
                <path
                  stroke-linecap="round"
                  stroke-linejoin="round"
                  stroke-width="2"
                  d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="px-6 pb-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              <p class="mb-3">
                Use the formula: <strong
                  >Throw Distance = Screen Width × Throw Ratio</strong>
              </p>
              <p class="mb-3">
                For example, a 100-inch diagonal 16:9 screen (87 inches wide)
                with a 1.5 throw ratio projector needs:
              </p>
              <p class="font-mono bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-700 p-2 rounded">
                87 × 1.5 = 130.5 inches (10.9 feet)
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details
            class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg shadow-sm overflow-hidden group border border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-700"
          >
            <summary
              class="px-6 py-4 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-200 flex justify-between items-center hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-700/50"
            >
              What is a good throw ratio for a projector?
              <svg
                class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 transform group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              >
                <path
                  stroke-linecap="round"
                  stroke-linejoin="round"
                  stroke-width="2"
                  d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="px-6 pb-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              <p class="mb-2">
                <strong>Standard throw ratio (1.5-1.8)</strong> is most versatile
                for home theaters:
              </p>
              <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-1 mb-3">
                <li>Best image quality per dollar</li>
                <li>Widest selection of models</li>
                <li>Good for 12-20 ft rooms</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                <strong>Ultra short throw (0.25-0.5)</strong> is best for small rooms.
                <strong>Short throw (0.8-1.2)</strong> balances flexibility and price.
                <strong>Long throw (2.0+)</strong> is for large venues.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details
            class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg shadow-sm overflow-hidden group border border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-700"
          >
            <summary
              class="px-6 py-4 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-200 flex justify-between items-center hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-700/50"
            >
              How far should a projector be from a 120 inch screen?
              <svg
                class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 transform group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              >
                <path
                  stroke-linecap="round"
                  stroke-linejoin="round"
                  stroke-width="2"
                  d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="px-6 pb-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              <p class="mb-3">
                For a <strong>120 inch screen</strong> (105 inches wide), placement
                distance depends on throw ratio:
              </p>
              <ul class="space-y-2">
                <li>• <strong>Ultra short throw (0.4):</strong> 3.5 feet</li>
                <li>• <strong>Short throw (0.8):</strong> 7.0 feet</li>
                <li>• <strong>Standard (1.5):</strong> 13.1 feet</li>
                <li>• <strong>Long throw (2.0):</strong> 17.5 feet</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details
            class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg shadow-sm overflow-hidden group border border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-700"
          >
            <summary
              class="px-6 py-4 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-200 flex justify-between items-center hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-700/50"
            >
              What's the difference between short throw and ultra short throw
              projectors?
              <svg
                class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 transform group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              >
                <path
                  stroke-linecap="round"
                  stroke-linejoin="round"
                  stroke-width="2"
                  d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="px-6 pb-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              <p class="mb-3">
                <strong>Ultra short throw (UST)</strong> projectors have 0.25-0.5
                throw ratio and sit <strong>8-20 inches</strong> from screen, ideal
                for tight spaces. Can sit on TV stand below screen.
              </p>
              <p>
                <strong>Short throw</strong> projectors have 0.8-1.2 ratio and need
                <strong>3-8 feet</strong> distance, offering better value than UST
                with good flexibility. Better for medium-sized rooms.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details
            class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg shadow-sm overflow-hidden group border border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-700"
          >
            <summary
              class="px-6 py-4 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-200 flex justify-between items-center hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-700/50"
            >
              What screen size is best for home theater?
              <svg
                class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 transform group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              >
                <path
                  stroke-linecap="round"
                  stroke-linejoin="round"
                  stroke-width="2"
                  d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7"></path>
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="px-6 pb-4 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
              <p class="mb-3">
                For home theaters, <strong>100-120 inch screens</strong> are the most
                popular choice:
              </p>
              <ul class="space-y-1 mb-3">
                <li>• <strong>100 inch:</strong> Works well for rooms 10-12 feet deep</li>
                <li>• <strong>120+ inches:</strong> Ideal for dedicated home theater rooms with 12-15+ feet seating distance</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                Consider wall width and ceiling height when choosing. A 120 inch 16:9
                screen requires at least 9.5 feet of wall width.
              </p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details
            class="bg-white dark:bg-gray-800 rounded-lg shadow-sm overflow-hidden group border border-gray-100 dark:border-gray-700"
          >
            <summary
              class="px-6 py-4 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-200 flex justify-between items-center hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-700/50"
            >
              Can I project on a wall instead of a screen?
              <svg
                class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-400 transform group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
